&style.oop;
public DateTimeDateTime::sub DateIntervalinterval
&style.procedural;
DateTimedate_sub DateTimeobject DateIntervalinterval
Modifies the specified DateTime object, by subtracting the specified DateInterval object.
A DateInterval built from an ISO 8601 duration string uses "wall-clock" arithmetic, while one obtained from DateTime::diff uses "civil" arithmetic: for the same nominal duration, the two can produce a result an hour apart once the subtraction crosses a DST transition, in either direction.
Like DateTimeImmutable::sub but works with DateTime.
The procedural version takes the DateTime object as its first argument.

&reftitle.changelog;





    &Version;
    &Description;





    8.3.0

     Now throws a
     DateInvalidOperationException with
     DateTime::sub, instead of
     a warning when an unsupported operation is attempted.
     date_sub has not been changed.

  &reftitle.examples;

   DateTime::sub example
   &style.oop;


<?php
$date = new DateTime('2000-01-20');
$date->sub(new DateInterval('P10D'));
echo $date->format('Y-m-d') . "\n";


   &example.outputs;


2000-01-10


   &style.procedural;


<?php
$date = date_create('2000-01-20');
date_sub($date, new DateInterval('P10D'));
echo date_format($date, 'Y-m-d') . "\n";


   &example.outputs;


2000-01-10




   Beware when subtracting months


<?php
$date = new DateTime('2001-04-30');
$interval = new DateInterval('P1M');

$date->sub($interval);
echo $date->format('Y-m-d') . "\n";

$date->sub($interval);
echo $date->format('Y-m-d') . "\n";


   &example.outputs;


2001-03-30
2001-03-02
